cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Searching for a Navier-Stokes Singularity, and Verifying That I Didn't Find One
message: >-
  If you use this software, the recorded results, or the findings catalogue,
  please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Paoli
    given-names: Samuele
repository-code: "https://github.com/PSamuele/navier-stokes-blowup-search"
abstract: >-
  A verification-driven computational search for finite-time singularities in the
  3D axisymmetric incompressible Navier-Stokes equations, confined to an
  engineered compressive domain. The study reports a null result: on two
  independent grids differing by a factor of two in every length, kinetic energy
  decreases strictly and the Beale-Kato-Majda integral stays bounded out to
  T = 0.55, with no evidence of singularity-consistent growth. Accompanying the
  result is a catalogue of five silent failure modes found in two earlier
  attempts by the same author - a mesh size field that degenerated to a constant,
  a vorticity diagnostic amplifying round-off by 1e14 on the symmetry axis, a
  breakdown threshold triggered by differentiated sampling noise, a stale
  momentum operator, and a projection step solved without boundary conditions -
  none of which raised an error, together with the regression tests that detect
  each. The repository also documents a grid convergence study whose own
  diagnostics reveal that the mesh was refined according to geometric intuition
  rather than the flow, leaving the wall boundary layer where the extreme
  vorticity actually lives resolved by under one element.
keywords:
  - computational fluid dynamics
  - Navier-Stokes equations
  - finite-time singularity
  - verification and validation
  - grid convergence index
  - axisymmetric flow
  - FEniCSx
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
